{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_660651092#1_1309334219","relevance":3,"reason":"This Up plot passage directly supports the answer: after Carl injures the construction worker, he is evicted by court order and ordered to move to a retirement home.","learned":"Some summaries add that the court order both evicts Carl from the house and orders him to move to a retirement home."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1802","reason":"The current query is still producing answer-bearing Up plot summaries with the key consequence, so continue to look for more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1802"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_1484694643#7_2613417333","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1802","rank":7,"snippet":"Animation Release UPisodes Home media Reception Critical response Box office Awards Video game Theme park attraction Real life Up house Gallery Videos Allusions External links Languages: \" , \"segment\": \"In the present, Carl is old and grumpy. He still lives in his same old house, which he feels is the thing that's keeping Ellie close to him, but surrounding it is a huge city landscape; new buildings are coming up all around <cover>Carl's property. A corporation is trying to move him, which would break what he had promised. He then ends up in a tussle with a construction worker over his broken mailbox and is forced by a court order to move into a retirement home. </cover>He then realizes this would be his last chance to fulfill that promise. The next morning, two nurses, A.J. and George, from the Shady Oaks Retirement Village come to pick up Carl. He comes out, but tells the workers that before he leaves, he wants to \"say one last goodbye to the old place\". They oblige and walk back towards the van. Suddenly, thousands and thousands of balloons appear from behind the house.\" , \"start_"}}
