{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ up carl \"retirement home\" balloons)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_414826521#2_997089089","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1837","rank":1,"snippet":"would take on the order of 20- to 30 million balloons to lift Carl's house. We ended up using 10,297 for most of the floating scenes, and 20,622 when it actually lifts off. The number varies from shot to shot, depending on the angle, the distance, and fine tuning the size so that it feels interesting, believable and visually simple. The Physics in Disney/Pixar's \"Up\" (Physics and Physicists blog) When a couple of people from a <cover>retirement home came to pick up Carl, he handed his bags to them and told them to wait for him while he said goodbye to his house. He then went in, and then deployed all the balloons </cover>that are attached to a base in his fireplace. All these balloons then started to pull on the house, breaking it off its foundation, and the house became afloat. But see, the balloons were already in the house. All Carl did was simply to \"unleash\" them so that they now float freely. But it doesn't mean that before then, they do not have the buoyancy.\" , \"start_char\": 1708.000000 , \"title\":"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_414826521#2_997089089","relevance":2,"reason":"This passage says people from a retirement home came to pick up Carl and he then launched his balloon house, but it does not connect that event to the court deeming him a public menace or ordering him to move.","learned":"A practical follow-up to the court/retirement-home consequence is that retirement-home staff come to pick Carl up before he escapes with the balloon house."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1837","reason":"The broader Up/Carl/retirement-home/balloons query may return plot summaries with more context tying the retirement-home pickup and balloon escape directly to the public-menace/court consequence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1837"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_414826521#1_997086581","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1837","rank":3,"snippet":"able to fly a house with balloons is pretty preposterous. We're not physicists, but one of our technical directors calculated that it would take on the order of 20- to 30 million balloons to lift Carl's house. We ended up using 10,297 for most of the floating scenes, and 20,622 when it actually lifts off. The number varies from shot to shot, depending on the angle, the distance, and fine tuning the size so that it feels interesting, believable and visually simple. The Physics in Disney/Pixar's \"Up\" (Physics and Physicists blog) When a couple of people from a <cover>retirement home came to pick up Carl, he handed his bags to them and told them to wait for him while he said goodbye to his house.\" , \"start_char\": 602.000000 , \"title\": \"Change of Subject: The physics of `Up' -- how many balloons </cover>would it really take to lift a house?\" , \"url\": \"https://blogs.chicagotribune.com/news_columnists_ezorn/2009/06/the-physics-of-up-how-many-balloons-would-it-really-take-to-lift-a-house.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_369552630#5_792749747","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
