{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"elderly carl fredricksen\" \"retirement home\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1255972390#2_2581768191","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1856","rank":2,"snippet":"Synopsis Contribute to This Page \" , \"segment\": \"— Jwelch5742 Determined to save his home and keep the promise he made to his wife, widower Carl Fredricksen embarks on a journey to the mysterious Paradise Falls in an airship of his own invention. Along the way he meets his childhood hero, forms a bond with a boy who has an absent father, and realizes the preciousness of the life he lived as well as the one he now lives. — David J. Rizzo After his wife's death and an incident at home, <cover>elderly Carl Fredricksen is forced into a retirement home, </cover>but he still wants to go on the South American adventure he and his wife had planned. Ultimately, the adventure involves a flying house, a young boy, a talking dog, and a large, strange bird. — grantss Little Carl Fredricksen is a dreamer who idolizes the adventurer Charles Muntz. When he meets Ellie, who also worships Muntz, they become close friends. However Charles Muntz falls into disgrace, accused of forging the skeleton of the monster of Paradise Falls. He travels in his blimp to South America to bring the monster back alive but is never seen again. ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1255972390#1_2581766740","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
